    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, there is a common way to define \"residuals\" more clearly, and it often involves a specific \"savings clause\" or \"general knowledge\" carve-out. In many software NDAs, the definition of \"residuals\" is explicitly limited to \"data byproducts\" or \"outputs generated directly from the processing of the Confidential Information,\" while simultaneously stating that \"general knowledge, skills, techniques, and methodologies acquired by the Receiving Party during the term of this Agreement shall remain the sole property of the Receiving Party.\"\n\nThis specific phrasing is crucial because it creates a legal firewall between the specific data you processed and the universal tools you learned to process it. Without this explicit definition, as we see in your current clause, the term \"residuals\" can be interpreted broadly by a court to include any derivative work, which is why the ambiguity you are feeling is a legitimate concern.\n\nTo ensure your skills remain yours, look for a definition that explicitly excludes \"general knowledge\" and \"industry-standard practices\" from the scope of \"residuals.\" If such a definition is missing, it is highly advisable to negotiate an amendment that inserts a sentence clarifying that \"residuals\" do not include \"any",
